    "mythology": {
      "body": "The ​stories of ‌**Shàngdì** ‌circulate within ​the Chinese ​corpus, ​where the ​figure ​embodies supreme ‌deity, ​heaven, sovereign.[^1]\n\n​### Primary ‌Attestation ​(Sources)\n\nShàngdì ‌is ​attested in ‌the ​canonical Chinese ​sources as ​a ​figure associated ​with ‌supreme deity, ​heaven, ‌sovereign.[^1]\n\n### ​Name ‌and Meaning ​(Etymology)\n\nThe ‌name ​means \"Highest ‌Emperor; ​the supreme ​god ‌of early ‌Chinese religion\" ‌and ​reflects the ​figure's ‌role in ​the ​tradition. **Shàngdì**, ‌the Supreme ​Lord, ​was the ‌high ​god of ‌the ​Shang and ​early Zhou ​dynasties. ​He received ‌sacrifices ‌from the ​Son ‌of Heaven ​and controlled ​harvest, ‌warfare, and ‌the ‌weather.[^1] Later ‌Chinese ​religion absorbed ‌Shàngdì into ​the ​more bureaucratic ​Tiāndào, ‌but the ​name ​retained its ​aura ‌of absolute, ​personal sovereignty. ​Oracle ​bones ask ‌whether ​Shàngdì will ‌grant ​rain, bless ‌the harvest, ​or ‌approve a ‌military ​campaign, revealing ​a ​direct, petitionary ​relationship between ‌king ‌and high ​god.[^1]",
